  "articleBody": "It can be super frustrating when you're putting out content but still not seeing your business cited by AI search engines like Google AI Overviews or ChatGPT. Traditional SEO often focused on keywords, but AI systems work a bit differently. They're looking for clear, structured data that directly answers user questions, and many businesses miss the mark here.\n\nFirst off, heavy website themes like WordPress or Wix, while great for design, can actually slow down or prevent AI crawlers from effectively getting to your content. AI systems prefer clean, low-latency HTML that's easy to parse. If your site is sluggish or overly complex, it's like putting up a 'Do Not Disturb' sign for AI bots.\n\nThen there's the content itself. Many businesses have thin, outdated, or just plain generic FAQ sections. AI wants highly specific, factual content that addresses the real questions people are asking on platforms like Quora, Reddit, or even Yelp Q&A. If your content doesn't directly hit those questions with precision and fact-density, AI is likely to pass it over.\n\nFinally, the critical piece often missing is structured data, or JSON-LD schema. This is how AI truly understands what your content is about and how it relates to your services. Without this, your helpful answers might just look like plain text to an AI system, making it hard for them to confidently cite your business.\n\n**What Can Change the Right Choice**\nChoosing an AI content strategy isn't one-size-fits-all. What works best depends on your current website's technical setup, how competitive your market is, and whether you're starting from scratch with AI visibility or trying to enhance existing efforts. Factors like the complexity of your services and the sheer volume of customer queries also play a huge role in determining the right approach for your business. It's about finding a solution that fits your specific needs, rather than just throwing content at the wall.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
